# Objectives and Endpoints {#sec-objectives-and-endpoints}

This protocol has two stages: Stage 1 focuses on algorithm development for the CRI biomarker; Stage 2 focuses on validation of the CRI-Opioid against its reference standard, the participant's concurrent cue-induced opioid wanting rating, with toxicology-confirmed opioid use as the tier-two anchor.

No CRI-Opioid or patient-reported cue-induced wanting rating reaches a treating clinician in either stage, so neither can influence clinical care or, through it, the outcomes the CRI-Opioid is validated against. A separate companion protocol focused on a clinician reader panel and a human-factors simulated-use study evaluates the potential impact of including these measures in care alongside standard measures.

## Study objectives

### Primary objective: Stage 1

**To fit the CRI to ERP data and concurrent cue-induced wanting ratings from adults with OUD, and lock it.** The Algorithm Lock Report is the committed deliverable of this stage.

### Primary objective: Stage 2

**To estimate the standalone accuracy of the locked CRI-Opioid against the pre-specified tier-one reference standard, the participant's cue-induced opioid wanting rating (@sec-reference-standard).** The locked CRI biomarker algorithm will be tested against unseen (held-out) cases and performance measures will be derived and reported. Toxicology-confirmed opioid use is the tier-two validation anchor and is carried as key secondaries.

### Secondary objectives

- **To estimate the incremental validity of the CRI-Opioid against toxicology-confirmed opioid use through 12 months**, over the participant's own cue-induced opioid wanting rating (@sec-incremental-validity-analysis).
- To describe opioid use, treatment status, and healthcare utilization over 12 months in both stages.
- To estimate the correlation between the CRI and cue-induced wanting rating.
- To characterize the psychometric properties of the CRI as descriptive analyses.

### Safety objectives

- To measure participant distress during cue exposure, as an alert rate and a stop rate, in both stages.
- To describe adverse events and device effects across both stages.

### Exploratory objectives

::: {.content-visible when-meta="tier-longitudinal"}
- To describe CRI-Opioid trajectory over 12 months in a sub-study of participants that will be followed-up longitudinally, and to model repetition and habituation effects across repeated exposures.
:::

- To generate additional hypotheses on the CRI-Opioid as a candidate dynamic biomarker for the prediction of clinical outcomes.

## Type I error rate control

The Stage 2 primary endpoint (Se and Sp of the locked CRI-Opioid against the tier-one reference standard, the cue-induced opioid wanting rating) carries a confirmatory alpha. Endpoints outside the Stage 2 primary fall into two kinds:

- **Decision rules.** The two Algorithm Lock criteria, model fit and repeatability (@sec-algorithm-lock), are pre-specified screening processes.
- **Descriptive estimates.** Everything else is reported as a point estimate with a 95% confidence interval (CI) and is not tested.

