## What the study's own probability of meeting this criterion is

At a true Se and Sp of 0.72, the probability of meeting full success is approximately **38%**, and between 31% and 41% across the assumptions the record leaves open about how strongly the reference standard clusters within a participant. At a true 0.75 it is approximately **72%**. These are simulated pass probabilities at the actual design, not expected confidence bounds.

This is the accepted cost of a one-shot design at a sample size the calendar fixes. Raising the goal without raising N converts a reachable study into one that fails unless the device performs better than anything on the record suggests; N cannot be raised. A blinded sample-size re-estimation makes an evaluability shortfall recoverable; it does not turn 38% into 90%, and nothing available at this N would.

Adopted by the sponsor on 2026-08-13. Before the pre-registration deposit, an assessment-level intraclass correlation outside 0.30–0.60, or a reference-positive fraction at the operative cut outside 0.35–0.65 in the deposit audit — a narrower band than the movement band at @sec-operative-cut, which governs the cut itself — recomputes the pass probabilities above; any resulting change to the criterion follows the amendment rule at @sec-operative-cut.
:::

### Secondary endpoints

Toxicology endpoints reflect FDA's recognized reference standard endpoints for therapeutic OUD devices. Given that the purpose of this protocol is not to evaluate the CRI-Opioid's therapeutic capacity, but rather its association with clinically relevant outcomes, we list toxicology endpoints as secondary and non-gating of the primary.

Additionally, the participant-clustered continuous / non-dichotomized association between the locked CRI and the concurrent cue-induced wanting rating, over all determinate assessments in the Stage 2 primary analysis set, will be estimated and reported.

| **Endpoint** | **Measure** | **Timepoint(s)** |
|------------------------|------------------------|------------------------|
| Toxicology-confirmed opioid use | Non-abstinence, classified per participant over the post-baseline toxicology results obtained (@sec-uds, @sec-incremental-validity-analysis) | Through 12 months |
| **Incremental validity of the CRI-Opioid** | Cross-validated discrimination of toxicology-confirmed opioid use by three models — CRI-Opioid alone, cue-induced opioid wanting rating alone, and both together — reported as AUC per model and as the ΔAUC attributable to the CRI-Opioid, each with a participant-clustered bootstrap 95% CI, under 5-fold participant-grouped cross-validation repeated 20 times (@sec-incremental-validity-analysis) | Through 12 months |
| Stimulant use | Amphetamine (AMP) and cocaine (COC) analytes | [1, 2, 3, 6, 12 months]{.content-visible when-meta="tier-outcomes"}[2, 3, 6, 9, 12 months]{.content-visible unless-meta="tier-outcomes"} |
| Within-participant concordance | Clustered concordance index (c-index): for pairs of assessments from the same participant, the probability that the locked CRI is higher for the assessment with the higher cue-induced wanting rating. | All determinate assessments in the Stage 2 primary analysis set; read at the Stage 2 primary database lock |

